  "note": "Run on the live AwwO, in the operator’s own account and workspace, on 2026-10-26, every agent on Claude Opus 5.5 working in its own sandbox computer. The film answers an Opus 5.5 video prompt by An Xuan (x.com/anxuanng/status/2110713337407574314, prompt 12622 of YouMind’s Opus 5.5 collection): a comparison in which the same brief made a steampunk spider in Blender and walked it in three.js. AwwO’s version is a steampunk mechanical wolf (AwwO is said “ah-woo”), modelled in code by one agent with no imported model or image texture, and walked by another. The canvas’s text was written for this film by the operator beforehand: the Director’s task (the prompt adapted to AwwO, with the facts the film may state and the look), the kernel contract every module writes to, each builder’s duty, and the tester’s and the editor’s tasks. What is seen and heard is what the canvas’s agents wrote. The canvas ran twice; the second run ran only the nodes it named, on the kept outputs of the rest. The first (10:59 to 11:59 UTC) delivered a film, which looked at before publishing was washed out: the timing sheet set the room environment’s light at 0.5, the number the operator’s own direction gave, and in a dark boiler room of polished brass that lit every pipe like a bright studio, so the pipes bloomed over the title and the wolf. The second (13:19 to 14:22) ran the kernel again with a task that sets that light to 0.08, over the sheet’s number, then the assembly, the tester, the editor, which cut the page into eight pieces of 2.5 s, and the edit, which rendered each piece from the page and recorded the page’s own sound (window.renderAudio), with no model. The page’s self-test could not finish in the tester’s browser, which draws three.js without a GPU at about 2.5 to 3.5 s a frame, so it reported no results; with the page’s timers stopped, as AwwO’s renderer runs them, the page became ready and every frame it drew settled. The canvas’s Review node ran beside the edit and is not part of this case.",
